  <article>
    <h1>48 Hours in Carmel-by-the-Sea</h1>
<figure>
  <img
    src="/images/carmel-beach-hero.jpg"
    alt="Carmel Beach and the Pacific coastline in Carmel-by-the-Sea"
  />
</figure>

    <p>
      <strong>
        Two days of storybook streets, hidden courtyards, California art,
        white-sand beach—and enough time to wander without turning Carmel
        into a checklist.
      </strong>
    </p>

    <p>Carmel-by-the-Sea isn't a place that rewards efficiency.</p>

    <p>That's part of its appeal.</p>

    <p>
      The village is small enough to walk, but interesting enough that
      walking directly from Point A to Point B almost misses the point.
    </p>

    <p>A narrow passageway disappears between buildings.</p>

    <p>A courtyard opens behind a storefront.</p>

    <p>
      A cottage looks as though someone illustrated it for a children's book.
    </p>

    <p>A gallery window makes you stop.</p>

    <p>
      And if you keep following Ocean Avenue downhill, eventually the
      buildings disappear and the Pacific takes over.
    </p>

    <p>
      Carmel has been attracting artists, writers, architects, and travelers
      for generations, and that history still influences how the village
      feels.
    </p>

    <p>
      There are destinations that ask us to drive farther, see more, and keep
      moving.
    </p>

    <p>Carmel asks something different.</p>

    <p><strong>Carmel asks us to wander.</strong></p>

    <h2>At a Glance</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>Best for:</strong> Couples, friends, art lovers, architecture
      enthusiasts, food travelers, walkers, wine lovers, and anyone who enjoys
      discovering small details rather than racing between major attractions.
    </p>

    <p><strong>The pace:</strong> Slow and highly walkable.</p>

    <p><strong>Stay:</strong> Two nights.</p>

    <p>
      <strong>Don't miss:</strong> Carmel's courtyards and passageways, its
      architecture and galleries, Carmel Beach, sunset, and enough unscheduled
      time to get slightly lost.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Good to know:</strong> Carmel-by-the-Sea is compact. If you stay
      in or near the village, much of this getaway can happen without your car.
    </p>

    <h2>Where to Stay</h2>

    <p>For this trip, <strong>stay in the village if you can</strong>.</p>

    <p>
      Carmel has inns, small hotels, historic properties, and more luxurious
      accommodations scattered throughout and around the village.
    </p>

    <p>
      But we're less concerned with a particular hotel than with what happens
      when you step outside.
    </p>

    <p>Can you walk to Ocean Avenue?</p>

    <p>Can you walk to dinner?</p>

    <p>
      Can you wander through town in the evening without retrieving the car?
    </p>

    <p>Can you walk down to the beach?</p>

    <p>If so, you've improved the entire weekend.</p>

    <p>
      This is one destination where paying somewhat more for location can
      change the experience.
    </p>

    <p>Park the car.</p>

    <p>Check in.</p>

    <p>Then start walking.</p>

    <h3>Check Carmel-by-the-Sea Hotel Availability</h3>

    <p>
      Compare current lodging options and availability for your travel dates.
    </p>

